   $q$What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the largest sphere that fits inside a rectangular box measuring 10 cm by 8 cm by 6 cm? Leave the answer in terms of $\pi$:$q$, $q$[{"text":"$12\\pi$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"squared-instead-of-cubed"},{"text":"$36\\pi$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$\\frac{500\\pi}{3}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-longest-dimension"},{"text":"$288\\pi$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-diameter-as-radius"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$36\pi$$q$, $q$The sphere is limited by the smallest dimension, so its diameter is 6 cm and its radius is 3 cm, giving $\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 3^3 = 36\pi$. The choice $\frac{500\pi}{3}$ uses a radius of 5 from the 10 cm side, but that sphere would not fit in the 6 cm direction.$q$, 'draft', null)
